  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free conditions will persist through the end \nof October\n\nA large area of high pressure will remain in place over the\nNortheast U.S. and Central Appalachians. Expect mainly clear \nskies and rain-free conditions right through the upcoming week.\n\nFog is a tough call for tonight. HREF and REFS probabilities are\nhighest around LNS and over the NW mtns. However, clouds moving\nin from the NW could keep the NW from getting too foggy. The\nbest bets are the same places as have been the last few nights\n(W Br Susq and Susq above Harrisburg, and Lake Raystown). Have\nmentioned patchy fog in many places due to the uncertainty, but\nkept dense fog mention to IPT/LNS.\n\nModerate variations will occur in daily and nocturnal\ntemperatures as at least one weak, dry cold fronts move SE \nacross the Commonwealth during the upcoming 5-6 days. Wind \ndirection/speed and cloud cover will also show some minor day- \nday variations. \n\nThe first of the aforementioned dry cold fronts will slip SE\nacross the CWA Monday night into early Tuesday, but with only \nlimited moisture to work with, just some periods of BKN, shallow\nstrato cu and cirrus will occur with no rainfall expected beyond\na few sprinkles over the Northern Mtns Monday night. Have kept\nthe PoPs below mentionable for now on Mon night. This front\nseems like it may slide in before it makes low clouds in the nrn\ntier. \n\nAfter a brief shot of seasonably cool air Tuesday and Wed \n(directed mainly at the NE half of the state), a late \nweek/Saturday rebound in temps (to well above normal values in \nthe low-mid 60s across the Mtns and upper 60s to mid 70s in the \nCentral and Southern Valleys) will follow. A second, weak\nfrontal boundary could brush by across the Northern tier\ncounties Friday afternoon and night. New Day8 is wet (40-60\nPoPs) as the pattern shifts to a more-zonal flow for the east.\nGFS is still dry, though. Models/members are not in as good of \nagreement from then onward.",
